# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

The connector is available only on Azure CXPs. Each instance is a vMX that
needs an authentication token from the Meraki Dashboard. A token can be used
once and expires about 1 hour after it is generated, so apply within that
hour. `auth_token` is stored in the Terraform state as a sensitive value.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/resources/alkira_connector_meraki_sdwan/resource.tf" }}

## Instances

- An instance is identified by its `host_name`. The order of the
  `instances` blocks doesn't matter, and any instance can be added or
  removed.
- A new `host_name` is a new instance: the old vMX is removed and a new vMX
  is deployed. Give it a new `auth_token`.
- The apply fails before any change if two instances have the same
  `host_name` or `auth_token`, or if an instance has the token that another
  instance had. Tokens can't be swapped between instances.
- `instances` is a set, so it can't be indexed. Use a `for` expression, for
  example
  `[for i in alkira_connector_meraki_sdwan.example.instances : i.wan_ip]`.

Changing `cxp`, `version`, `tunnel_protocol` or `target_segment.segment_id`
after the connector is provisioned is not supported.

## Replacing an Authentication Token

Set a new `auth_token` on the instance. The plan shows the instance as
replaced, but the apply keeps the instance. The instance credential can't
be changed, so the apply creates a credential with the new token and
updates the connector to use it. `credential_id` changes. The provision
redeploys that vMX only.

- The redeploy needs `provision = true` on the provider. Without it, the vMX
  is redeployed at the next provision of the network.
- Replace the token of one instance per apply to keep the hub up.
- The instance's tunnels are down until the new vMX registers with the
  Meraki Dashboard. If the new vMX gets a different `wan_ip`, set the local
  ID of its peers in the Meraki Dashboard to the new `wan_ip`.
- If the vMX isn't redeployed, provision the network again.
- If the connector update fails, the apply deletes the new credentials
  that the connector doesn't use (none, if it can't read the connector),
  and the state keeps the old token. Apply
  again to retry. If the connector saved the update before the error, the
  state keeps the new token instead (or, if the apply can't read the
  connector, the next refresh adopts the saved credential), and the token
  isn't sent again.
- If the connector is in FAILED state and the provider provisions, the
  backend keeps the saved configuration. The apply fails and names the
  instances that weren't updated. See below.

The API never returns the token, so Terraform tracks the instance's
`credential_id`. If the connector has another credential than the state
(a token changed outside Terraform, or an apply that stopped after the
update), the refresh clears the token in the state, and the plan shows the
instance as changed. The next apply keeps the connector's credential, sends
no token, and shows the warning "AUTH TOKEN NOT SENT". Nothing is
redeployed, and a spent token is never sent again. Change `auth_token`
after that to replace the token.

### Replacing a Token on a FAILED Connector

A FAILED connector with `provision = true` provisions its saved
configuration and ignores changes. To replace a spent or expired token:

1. Set the new `auth_token`.
2. Apply with `provision = false` on the provider. The connector saves the
   new credential. Nothing is deployed.
3. Provision the network from the Alkira portal. The connector is
   provisioned with the new token. An apply with `provision = true`
   provisions only when its plan shows a change.

